{"from": "claim:lk-14-05-son-or-ox-well", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-12-11-sheep-in-pit", "note": "Luke's Sabbath example is a son or an ox fallen into a well, pulled out immediately; Matthew's is one sheep fallen into a pit, lifted out - the same accepted Sabbath-rescue argument with different examples (reviewer 7 asserts-audit fix)."}
{"from": "claim:lk-14-16-17-great-supper-servants", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-22-02-04-wedding-feast-invited", "note": "Luke's host is 'a certain man' giving a great supper; Matthew's is a king giving a marriage feast for his son - the same invited-guests-called-by-servants structure with different hosts and settings (reviewer 7 asserts-audit fix)."}
{"from": "text:damascus-document-11-13-14", "type": "complicates", "to": "claim:lk-14-05-son-or-ox-well", "note": "The Damascus Document forbids raising an animal that has fallen into a pit or ditch on the Sabbath - in tension with the immediate rescue Jesus's argument assumes as accepted practice; it shows Sabbath animal rescue was disputed among Second Temple Jewish groups. (The same text carries a C-grade debate edge on the 13:15 watering claim.)"}
